= Framing effect =

== Overview ==
A '''framing effect''' occurs when judgments or choices change because equivalent or closely related information is presented in different frames.

Frames can emphasize gains versus losses, positive versus negative attributes, or different goals and consequences.

== Risky-choice framing ==
A classic example presents formally equivalent outcomes as lives saved or lives lost.

People often show more risk aversion under gain frames and more risk seeking under loss frames.

This pattern helped motivate prospect theory.

== Attribute framing ==
The same attribute can be described positively or negatively.

For example, “90% lean” and “10% fat” describe the same proportion but can produce different evaluations.

== Goal framing ==
Messages can emphasize the benefits of performing an action or the costs of not performing it.

Goal framing is conceptually different from risky-choice framing even though both use positive/negative presentation.

== Framing and representation ==
Framing effects do not always mean participants ignore equivalence.

A frame can change which features, reference points, goals, or interpretations are psychologically represented.

Therefore:

<pre>
framing effect ≠ any wording effect
</pre>

== Rationality ==
Some framing effects violate invariance principles in normative decision theory.

In real communication, however, wording can also legitimately convey pragmatic information, so experiments must distinguish pure reformulation from changed meaning.

== Related concepts ==
[[Cognitive bias]] and [[Probability]] provide the judgment and decision context for framing, especially where risky choice is involved.

